          "evidence": "Step 3.1 proves the forward direction of the unique-minimum statement: two elements of ${}^IW^J$ in one double coset satisfy $d'=udv$ with $\\ell(d')=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)$, hence are equal."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 combined with step 3.1 proves the reverse direction: the minimum of $W_IdW_J$ is an element of ${}^IW^J$ (by [F3]) and the normal form applies to it, so minimum and transversal element coincide."
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-cg-infinite-dihedral-parabolic-double-cosets": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $m<\\infty$ and $q\\le m$, or if $m=\\infty$ and $q\\ge1$, then $\\ell(w_q)=q$, every word in $S$ representing $w_q$ has length at least $q$, and $w_1,\\dots,w_q$ are pairwise distinct.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$W^I:=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\}=\\{w\\in W:D_R(w)\\cap I=\\emptyset\\},$$\n\n$${}^IW:=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(sw)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\}=\\{w\\in W:D_L(w)\\cap I=\\emptyset\\}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The set of **reflections** of $W$ is $T:=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}\\subset W$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cg-double-coset-unique-minimum-and-normal-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) Unique minimum.** Two elements of ${}^IW^J$ lie in a common double coset\n$W_IwW_J$ only if they are equal. Hence, by\n[[lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality]] (1),(2), every double\ncoset $W_IwW_J$ contains exactly one element of ${}^IW^J$, namely its unique\nelement of minimum length; in particular $d$ is uniquely determined by the\ndouble coset $W_IdW_J$.\n\n**(2) The additive normal form.** Every $x\\in W_IdW_J$ has a unique\nrepresentation\n\n$$x=u\\,d\\,v\\qquad\\text{with }u\\in W_I^K,\\ v\\in W_J,$$\n\nand every product $udv$ with $u\\in W_I^K$, $v\\in W_J$ lies in $W_IdW_J$. Thus\n\n$$W_I^K\\times W_J\\to W_IdW_J,\\qquad (u,v)\\mapsto udv$$\n\nis a bijection, and for all $u\\in W_I^K$ and $v\\in W_J$\n\n$$\\ell(udv)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(v).$$\n\n**(3) The size of a double coset.** $|W_IdW_J|=|W_I^K|\\cdot|W_J|$; and if $W_I$\nis finite then $|W_IdW_J|=|W_I|\\,|W_J|/|W_K|$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$W_J=\\langle J\\rangle=\\{w\\in W:S(w)\\subseteq J\\}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For every group $G$ and every map $f:S\\to G$ with $f(s)^2=1$ for all $s\\in S$ and $(f(s)f(t))^{m(s,t)}=1$ in $G$ for all $s\\ne t$ with $m(s,t)<\\infty$, there is a unique group homomorphism $\\varphi:W\\to G$ with $\\varphi(s)=f(s)$ for all $s\\in S$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-group-homomorphism",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Condition (H2) is not imposed for groups because it follows: a group homomorphism automatically satisfies $f(e) = e'$ and $f(x^{-1}) = f(x)^{-1}$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) Intersections.** For all $I,J\\subseteq S$,\n\n$$W_I\\cap W_J=W_{I\\cap J}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The parabolic subgroups of $W$ are exactly $\\{1\\}$, the order-two subgroups $\\{1,r\\}$ with $r\\in T$, and $W$; $W_I\\cap W_J=\\{1\\}$, and $W_I$, $W_J$ are not conjugate, by the parity homomorphism $\\varphi:W\\to\\mathbb Z/2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Normal form $W=\\{u^m\\}\\cup\\{u^ms\\}$ with the two families disjoint; the case-by-case comparison of reduced alternating forms gives ${}^IW=\\{u^m:m\\le0\\}\\cup\\{u^ms:m\\le-1\\}$ and $W^J=\\{u^m:m\\le0\\}\\cup\\{u^ms:m\\ge0\\}$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $d_k=(ts)^k=u^{-k}$ the double coset is $W_I(ts)^kW_J=\\{u^{-k},u^ks,u^{-(k+1)}s,u^{k+1}\\}$ with lengths $2k,2k+1,2k+1,2k+2$, pairwise distinct, so its minimum is $d_k\\in{}^IW^J$ and by [F4] it is the unique minimum.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Intersecting the two lists of step 1.2 gives ${}^IW^J=\\{u^m:m\\le0\\}=\\{u^{-k}:k\\ge0\\}=\\{(ts)^k:k\\ge0\\}$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "$d_k^{-1}sd_k=u^{2k}s\\notin\\{1,t\\}$, so $K=\\emptyset$, $W_I^K=W_I$ and $|W_I(ts)^kW_J|=4$; the four families $u^{-k}$, $u^{k+1}$, $u^ks$, $u^{-(k+1)}s$ partition the two normal-form families, so the double cosets $W_I(ts)^kW_J$, $k\\ge0$, partition $W$, the first being $\\{1,s,t,st\\}$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.2 includes $k=0$ with $(ts)^0=1$ in ${}^IW^J$, and step 3.1 obtains the first block $W_IW_J=\\{1,s,t,st\\}$; the empty word normal form $u^0=1$ is used in step 1.2."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 uses $\\ell(u^m)=2|m|$, in particular $\\ell(1)=0$; step 3.1's partition includes $u^0$ and $u^0s$ in the first block, i.e. the zero-index entries of both families."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 treats the order-two parabolics $\\{1,s\\}$, $\\{1,t\\}$ and the rank-one subgroups $\\{1,r\\}$; step 1.2 computes the length-one elements $s=u^0s$ and $t=u^{-1}s$."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The edge $m(s,t)=\\infty$ is the degenerate case, handled in step 1.2 by the normal form and [F1]; no finite dihedral order $m<\\infty$ is used anywhere."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example works in a fixed infinite dihedral group; it contains no interval or ordered parameter family with endpoints, the index $k\\ge0$ being an unbounded family without an upper endpoint."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "No Choice is used: step 1.2 uses the alternating normal form, step 2.2 is a set intersection of the two explicit lists, and step 2.1 identifies the unique minimum by comparing four lengths."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 proves the forward direction of the list descriptions: each element $u^m$ with $m\\ge1$ has a descent $s$, and each $u^ms$ with $m\\le-1$ has a descent $t$, so they are excluded from ${}^IW$ and $W^J$ respectively."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 also proves the reverse direction element by element: for the surviving indices $s\\,u^m$ and $u^ms\\,s$ are alternating words of length exceeding $\\ell$, so no descent occurs and the elements lie in ${}^IW$ respectively $W^J$."
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-cg-s4-coset-minima-and-double-coset-decomposition": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $s_i\\mapsto(i\\ i+1)$ extends to an isomorphism $W\\to S_n$ (the letters $1,\\dots,n$ carry the library's symmetric group by the order-preserving identification with $\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$, under which $(i\\ i+1)$ is the adjacent transposition $(i-1\\ i)$), and for every $w\\in W$,\n$$\\ell(w)=\\operatorname{inv}\\bigl(\\varphi(w)\\bigr),$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$${}^IW^J:={}^IW\\cap W^J=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(sw)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\ \\text{ and }\\ \\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in J\\};$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) Intersections.** For all $I,J\\subseteq S$,\n\n$$W_I\\cap W_J=W_{I\\cap J}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cg-double-coset-unique-minimum-and-normal-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) Unique minimum.** Two elements of ${}^IW^J$ lie in a common double coset\n$W_IwW_J$ only if they are equal. Hence, by\n[[lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality]] (1),(2), every double\ncoset $W_IwW_J$ contains exactly one element of ${}^IW^J$, namely its unique\nelement of minimum length; in particular $d$ is uniquely determined by the\ndouble coset $W_IdW_J$.\n\n**(2) The additive normal form.** Every $x\\in W_IdW_J$ has a unique\nrepresentation\n\n$$x=u\\,d\\,v\\qquad\\text{with }u\\in W_I^K,\\ v\\in W_J,$$\n\nand every product $udv$ with $u\\in W_I^K$, $v\\in W_J$ lies in $W_IdW_J$. Thus\n\n$$W_I^K\\times W_J\\to W_IdW_J,\\qquad (u,v)\\mapsto udv$$\n\nis a bijection, and for all $u\\in W_I^K$ and $v\\in W_J$\n\n$$\\ell(udv)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(v).$$\n\n**(3) The size of a double coset.** $|W_IdW_J|=|W_I^K|\\cdot|W_J|$; and if $W_I$\nis finite then $|W_IdW_J|=|W_I|\\,|W_J|/|W_K|$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-cg-double-coset-intersection-parabolic",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) The intersection.** $W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}=W_K$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "By inversion ($w\\mapsto w^{-1}$ preserves lengths and interchanges the two coset families $\\{W_Ja\\}$ and $\\{aW_J\\}$), every right coset $aW_J:=\\{au:u\\in W_J\\}$ has a unique minimal element $d$, characterized by $\\ell(ds)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$ and satisfying $\\ell(du)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(u)$ for all $u\\in W_J$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "$W_I=\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle$ consists of the permutations with $w(4)=4$ and $W_J$ of those with $w(1)=1$, both of order $6$; their intersection is $\\{1234,1324\\}=W_{\\{s_2\\}}=W_{I\\cap J}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "*The quotients and the two double cosets.* Right multiplication by $s_i$ swaps the entries of the one-line form in positions $i$ and $i+1$, so it changes the inversion number by $\\pm1$, and decreases it exactly when those two entries are in decreasing order, that is when $w(i)>w(i+1)$; by [F1] the same holds for $\\ell$. Left multiplication by $s_i$ swaps the values $i$ and $i+1$ wherever they occur, so it decreases the inversion number exactly when the value $i$ occurs after the value $i+1$, that is when $w^{-1}(i)>w^{-1}(i+1)$. Hence $w\\in W^J$ iff $w(2)<w(3)$ and $w(3)<w(4)$, which for the $24$ permutations gives $W^J=\\{1234,2134,3124,4123\\}$, and $w\\in{}^IW$ iff $w^{-1}(1)<w^{-1}(2)$ and $w^{-1}(2)<w^{-1}(3)$, which gives ${}^IW=\\{1234,1243,1423,4123\\}$. Intersecting, ${}^IW^J=\\{1234,4123\\}$. By [F4] each double coset contains exactly one element of ${}^IW^J$, so the two double cosets $W_I\\,1234\\,W_J$ and $W_I\\,4123\\,W_J$ are distinct and their union is all of $S_4$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "For $I=\\{s_1\\}$, $J=\\{s_3\\}$: ${}^IW^J$ has the seven listed elements, the seven double cosets have sizes $4,4,4,4,4,2,2$, $K=\\emptyset$ for the first five minima and $K=\\{s_1\\}$ for $d=3412,4312$, with $W_I3412W_J=\\{3412,3421\\}$ and $W_I4123W_J=\\{4123,4132,4213,4231\\}$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The double cosets of $1234$ and $4123$ have orders $18$ and $6$; for $d=1234$ one has $K=\\{s_2\\}$ and $W_I^K=\\{1234,2134,3124\\}$, for $d=4123$ one has $d^{-1}s_1d=s_2$, $d^{-1}s_2d=s_3$, $K=\\{s_1,s_2\\}$ and $W_I^K=\\{1234\\}$, matching $|W_I^K|\\cdot|W_J|$ and the finite formula.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "*Coset minima and the normal form of $2143$.* Let $w=2143$, of length $2$ by [F1]. Multiplying the six elements of $W_I$ into $w$ gives $W_Iw=\\{1243,1342,2143,2341,3142,3241\\}$, whose element of least length is $1243=s_3$, of length $1$, and this is the unique element of ${}^IW$ in the left coset by step 1.2; multiplying the six elements of $W_J$ on the right gives $wW_J=\\{2134,2143,2314,2341,2413,2431\\}$, whose element of least length is $2134=s_1$, of length $1$, the unique element of $W^J$ in the right coset by step 1.2. Since $2143=2134\\cdot1243$ and $2134\\in W_I$, $1243\\in W_J$, the element $2143$ lies in the double coset of $d=1234$, whose minimum is $1234$ by step 2.1; the representation in the normal form of [F4] for $K=\\{s_2\\}$ is $2143=2134\\cdot1234\\cdot1243$, with $2134\\in W_I^K=\\{1234,2134,3124\\}$ by step 2.1 and $1243\\in W_J$, and the lengths add: $\\ell(2134)+\\ell(1234)+\\ell(1243)=1+0+1=2=\\ell(2143)$, in agreement with [F4] and [F6]. This completes the example.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.3 includes ${}^IW^J\\ni1234$ and the trivial double coset element $1$; step 2.1 lists $1234$ as the minimum of the double coset of order $18$, i.e. the case of an empty descent set."
        },
        {
